WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.900
No risk, no reward, right? But what happens when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.380
a major network risks millions&nbsp;
on a show about musical cops?

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:13.200
Developed by Lana and Lilly Wachowski along&nbsp;
with "Babylon 5" creator J. Michael Straczynski,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.340
"Sense8" premiered on Netflix in&nbsp;
2015. The show follows a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.580
people from around the world who&nbsp;
inexplicably gain the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.300
psychically link with each other. Before&nbsp;
they know it, they're being menaced by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.740
secret organization apparently led by an&nbsp;
enigmatic individual known as "Whispers."

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.960
"Sense8" received positive reviews from both&nbsp;
critics and viewers and won a number of awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.940
but that didn't stop Netflix from canceling&nbsp;
the show at the end of Season 2. An outcry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.060
from fans led to the streaming giant&nbsp;
producing a final two-and-a-half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.960
hour-long finale to tie up the story and&nbsp;
ensure it didn't end on a cliffhanger.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.420
Variety reported that Ted Sarandos, chief content&nbsp;
officer for Netflix at the time, confirmed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:56.100
show was simply proving to be too expensive&nbsp;
considering its small audience. While the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.240
doesn't generally release information about&nbsp;
budgets and viewing figures, the fact that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.940
cost around $9 million per episode was seemingly&nbsp;
too much for Netflix to justify. Most of that cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.500
came down to the long production time and the fact&nbsp;
that it was filmed on location around the world.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.120
Netflix is no stranger to canceling&nbsp;
high-profile shows if they don't live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.020
up to the company's expectations — which&nbsp;
could mean they haven't managed to attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.920
enough viewers. It shouldn't come as a huge&nbsp;
surprise that "The Get Down" was axed after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:28.320
just a single season despite rave reviews&nbsp;
and numerous award nominations. Created by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.080
director Baz Luhrmann and Pulitzer Prize-winning&nbsp;
playwright Stephen Adly Guirgis, the 2016 musical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.660
drama focuses on a group of teenagers living in&nbsp;
the South Bronx of New York during the 1970s.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.640
Netflix announced that "The Get Down" would not&nbsp;
be returning to the streaming service in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.580
having already cut down the first season from&nbsp;
13 episodes to 11. This was largely a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:56.040
of costly production delays and the mid-season&nbsp;
departure of showrunner Shawn Ryan. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.840
Variety, research suggested that the show received&nbsp;
just a fraction of the viewers as other Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.240
originals and interest appeared to drop off&nbsp;
quickly. Considering the first season apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.120
cost around $120 million, it's little wonder that&nbsp;
Netflix did not want to commit to more episodes.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.720
"What'd you think, you were just gonna keep&nbsp;
running this thing without my knowledge?"&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:21.840
"Information regarding the Mount Inostranka&nbsp;
facility has always been on a need to know basis."

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:28.080
"The Event" is an NBC sci-fi thriller series that&nbsp;
first hit television screens in 2010 and ran for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.240
single season before it was unceremoniously&nbsp;
shelved after just 22 episodes. The story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.440
focuses on a group of extraterrestrials who&nbsp;
crash-land on Earth in a remote region of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.480
Alaska during World War II. Those onboard the&nbsp;
unidentified craft are taken prisoner and held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.400
in captivity by the United States, although many&nbsp;
survivors evade capture and manage to hide among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.600
the human population. When a new president&nbsp;
takes office, he plans to free the aliens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.520
and reveal their existence to the world but is&nbsp;
quickly caught up in an assassination attempt.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.400
Even though "The Event" had a strong&nbsp;
start when it was first broadcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.960
it quickly began to lose viewers. The&nbsp;
series also failed to impress critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.000
making it a likely contender for cancellation.&nbsp;
While an exact budget has never been revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.680
The Hollywood Reporter indicated that&nbsp;
NBC spent at least $10 million marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.440
"The Event," suggesting that it&nbsp;
was a big-budget undertaking.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.200
When most people think of "The Fugitive," they&nbsp;
likely picture the 1993 film starring Harrison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.300
Ford and Tommy Lee Jones. Dr. Richard Kimble is&nbsp;
framed for the murder of his wife and sentenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.960
to death for killing her. He's inadvertently&nbsp;
freed, however, while being transported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.880
to prison and sets out to find the actual&nbsp;
culprit while being pursued by U.S. marshals.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.320
There have been multiple versions of the story&nbsp;
over the years, dating back to the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.740
1960s television series. In 2000, CBS launched&nbsp;
a remake of the earlier show, also titled "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.660
Fugitive," with Tim Daly portraying Kimble&nbsp;
and Mykelti Williamson as the U.S. marshal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.920
trying to apprehend him. It was canceled&nbsp;
after one season, ending on a cliffhanger.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.320
The Post Gazette reported that the pilot&nbsp;
alone cost an estimated $6 million to produce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:05.400
a rather large figure for 2000.&nbsp;
According to The Hollywood Reporter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.120
drama pilots cost $5 million on average in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.680
suggesting that "The Fugitive" was certainly&nbsp;
on the high scale for a television series.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:19.020
Although it is not part of the official canon&nbsp;
of the "Star Wars" universe, the "The Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:24.180
Holiday Special" came out not long after the first&nbsp;
film and is set between "A New Hope" and "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:29.340
Empire Strikes Back." It centers on Chewbacca and&nbsp;
Han Solo's attempt to visit the planet Kashyyyk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.800
while being pursued by Imperial forces and&nbsp;
bounty hunter Boba Fett. The $1 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.060
which was around 10% of the total cost&nbsp;
of "A New Hope," is equivalent to more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:44.160
than $4.5 million today, so the special&nbsp;
was certainly an expensive production.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.960
The show was universally panned by almost&nbsp;
everyone who has seen it. Even those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.340
who worked on the project, including cast&nbsp;
members such as Mark Hamill, Carrie Fisher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.940
and Harrison Ford, have been critical&nbsp;
of the project and how it turned out.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:03.060
"Do you remember making this Christmas special?&nbsp;
I think it was 1978. No, you don't remember it?"

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.080
"Star Wars" creator and director&nbsp;
George Lucas has even remarked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.020
that he wants to destroy every copy of&nbsp;
the special that is still available.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:16.620
Starring Natalie Martinez, Kate Bosworth,&nbsp;
Kyle Schmid, and Sibylla Deen, "The I-Land"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.240
is a Netflix sci-fi thriller that tracks 10&nbsp;
strangers who wake up on a beach without any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:25.620
idea how they got there or knowledge about&nbsp;
their own identities. Conflict soon breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.000
out and it is later revealed that this is&nbsp;
a digital simulation meant to test whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.140
violent criminals will return to the way they&nbsp;
once behaved under all new circumstances.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.160
After its premiere, "The I-Land"&nbsp;
was universally criticized. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.140
holds a critics' rating of just 8% on Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes and a similarly bad audience score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.940
suggesting that very few people enjoyed&nbsp;
anything about it. In its review of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.800
Paste criticized its absurdity and called&nbsp;
it an embarrassment; the writer stated:

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.400
"I have watched some truly,&nbsp;
truly bad series in my day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.340
but few that went off the&nbsp;
rails this hard this fast."

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:04.260
Considering that "The I-Land" cost&nbsp;
approximately $14 million for a limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.580
series of just seven 40-minute episodes,&nbsp;
few could argue this was a good investment.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.980
2018's "Troy: Fall of a City" was a&nbsp;
co-production with Netflix and the BBC.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:19.140
Set during the mythological age of ancient Greece,&nbsp;
the show charts the Trojan invasion after Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:23.640
played by Louis Hunter, flees Sparta with&nbsp;
Bella Dayne's Helen of Troy. This prompts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:27.780
a war between the two nations as Achilles and&nbsp;
the other Greeks attempt to take back Helen.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:32.940
Despite positive reviews, "Troy: Fall of a&nbsp;
City" mostly failed to attract viewers. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:38.160
managed just 3.2 million audience members for&nbsp;
its opening episode, almost 2 million less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.960
a typical Saturday evening primetime slot. A&nbsp;
few episodes later, this number had dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.660
to just 1.6 million in total — a dramatic fall&nbsp;
for an expensive show. According to The Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:55.680
the series cost almost $2.5 million an episode,&nbsp;
putting it at a total cost of $20 million for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.960
eight-episode first season. This made it one&nbsp;
of the BBC's most expensive dramas ever made.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:08.280
An adaptation of Channel 4's 2013 series of the&nbsp;
same name, "Utopia" is a 2020 Amazon Prime Video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:13.980
series that was created by "Gone Girl" writer&nbsp;
Gillian Flynn. Starring John Cusack, Rainn Wilson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:19.080
Sasha Lane, and Desmin Borges, the premise&nbsp;
of the show is that a group of young adults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:24.600
discover a cult comic book and become aware of&nbsp;
its connection to a vast global conspiracy. Armed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:28.800
with this knowledge, they set out to save the&nbsp;
world but also become targets for a secretive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.540
underground organization. Amazon canceled the&nbsp;
series after a single eight-episode season.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:37.920
"Utopia" was originally set to be&nbsp;
produced by HBO, but the show was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.480
shelved following a dispute over the budget.&nbsp;
According to rumors reported by Deadline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:46.920
director David Fincher wanted more&nbsp;
than $100 million to shoot the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:51.660
but executives at HBO were unwilling&nbsp;
to offer more than $95 million.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:57.120
Although the series found a new home with Amazon,&nbsp;
in light of COVID-19's all-encompassing impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.740
on the real world of 2020, it's safe to&nbsp;
say the TV-viewing public of the era was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:07.440
uniquely disinterested in an escapist sci-fi&nbsp;
fantasy program about a deadly global pandemic.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.420
"Cop Rock" was created by Steven Bochco,&nbsp;
the man responsible for the hit crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:16.260
drama "Hill Street Blues." But "Cop&nbsp;
Rock" takes the concept of a typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.060
police procedural and blends in musical&nbsp;
elements. As the various detectives and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:25.080
police officers in the LAPD carry out&nbsp;
their work, a number of choreographed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.160
musical numbers push the story forward&nbsp;
and provide extra context to viewers.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:30.720
"There's gonna be singing and dancing."&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.720
"About police?"
"Yeah, about police."

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:39.240
As a highly experimental concept, "Cop Rock"&nbsp;
was always likely to face an uphill battle and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:43.980
the show was canceled after just 11 episodes of&nbsp;
the planned 13 that were due to make up Season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:48.900
1. Reports from the time suggest that the series&nbsp;
failed to attract a large enough audience and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:54.780
average reviews. The New York Times revealed that&nbsp;
"Cop Rock" also had a large budget — roughly 40%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:59.940
larger than equivalent shows in its time slot&nbsp;
and was costing more than $1 million per week.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:06.540
"The Playboy Club" is a 2011 NBC series set&nbsp;
during the 1960s that follows the action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.580
around the first Playboy Club in Chicago. The&nbsp;
show stars Eddie Cibrian as shady lawyer Nick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:15.540
Dalton and Amber Heard as a new employee at&nbsp;
the club with a closet full of skeletons.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.620
Controversial from the moment that it first&nbsp;
hit television screens, "The Playboy Club"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:24.120
did not prove to be a hit with critics or&nbsp;
viewers. The series struggled in the ratings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:29.100
losing millions of viewers between the first and&nbsp;
third episodes, prompting NBC to cancel the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:33.540
mid-season. Showrunner Chad Hodge confirmed&nbsp;
that "The Playboy Club" had four completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:39.300
episodes that remain unaired, despite the series&nbsp;
costing NBC at least $2 million an episode.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.700
"Friends" was one of the biggest shows of&nbsp;
the '90s. When it finally came to an end in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:49.260
2004 after 10 seasons, it made perfect&nbsp;
sense that NBC would want to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:53.700
its success in some way with a spin-off.&nbsp;
The new series, which made its debut near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.980
the end of 2004 and was titled simply "Joey"&nbsp;
focused entirely on Matt LeBlanc's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:03.420
Joey Tribbiani, who moves to Hollywood&nbsp;
to try and make it as a movie actor.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:08.880
The concept made sense, but the execution left&nbsp;
a lot to be desired. Kevin Bright, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.920
original "Friends" producers, believed that NBC&nbsp;
had disregarded his ideas for the spin-off and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.420
made the show less fun than it should have been.&nbsp;
Ratings plummeted from the first episode onward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:23.400
and NBC chose to cancel the series midway&nbsp;
through its Season 2. Although official figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:28.260
about the cost of making "Joey" have never been&nbsp;
detailed, U.K. channel FIVE was paying as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:34.620
£500,000 for each episode, a staggering five&nbsp;
times more than the usual license fee. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:38.220
LeBlanc claims that he earned $30&nbsp;
million for his role in the spin-off.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:44.760
Based on the 2013 book "The Good Girls Revolt" by&nbsp;
Lynn Povich, "Good Girls Revolt" tells the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:49.800
of a group of female writers at a news magazine&nbsp;
who find themselves subjected to absurd amounts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:55.800
sexism. Genevieve Angelson, Anna Camp, Erin Darke,&nbsp;
and Hunter Parrish were part of the main cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:00.660
and the show was sometimes compared to the highly&nbsp;
acclaimed "Mad Men" by critics and fans alike.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:04.140
Unfortunately, the high quality&nbsp;
didn't translate into viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:10.380
with Reuters revealing that only 1.6 million tuned&nbsp;
in. With a budget in the region of $80 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.740
it is little wonder that Amazon&nbsp;
chose not to renew for Season 2.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:18.780
A collaboration between Martin Scorsese,&nbsp;
Terence Winter, and Mick Jagger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:23.760
"Vinyl" is a 2016 HBO series that focuses&nbsp;
on the music scene in New York during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:29.580
1970s. As the tastes of the population shift from&nbsp;
rock to disco and hip-hop music, a record company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:34.560
executive does his best to stay relevant in&nbsp;
the shifting marketplace. Bobby Cannavale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:38.880
Ato Essandoh, Jack Quaid, and&nbsp;
Ray Romano had starring roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.440
while Scorsese himself executive produced&nbsp;
and directed the two-hour long pilot.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.620
"Do you have any idea how f---in' huge that is?"

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:52.560
Before Episode 1 even hit TV screens, HBO had&nbsp;
given the greenlight for Season 2. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:57.480
network quickly reversed course. Only months&nbsp;
after the show's first season finale aired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:01.440
HBO confirmed that "Vinyl" had instead&nbsp;
been canceled as it "failed to land."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:06.600
Costing $100 million for the first season,&nbsp;
that accounts to $10 million per episode.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:12.600
It was one of HBO's most expensive projects, but&nbsp;
also failed to find an audience and managed less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.920
than a million viewers on average. Scorsese,&nbsp;
however, believes that the series could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:21.420
been more successful if he had directed all&nbsp;
the episodes and devoted more time to it.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.820
Despite plenty of criticism about its final&nbsp;
season, "Game of Thrones" was such a hit that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:32.040
HBO was always likely to continue the franchise&nbsp;
in some way. Fans eventually got more Westeros&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:35.940
with the popular spin-off series "House of&nbsp;
the Dragon," but long before that another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:39.840
project was in the works. Known by its&nbsp;
production name "Bloodmoon," this show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:43.260
was set to take place thousands of years&nbsp;
before the events of "Game of Thrones."

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:49.500
HBO officially greenlit a pilot for the series&nbsp;
in 2018 and cast actors such as Naomi Watts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:55.620
in key roles. With a budget of $30 million, the&nbsp;
"Bloodmoon" pilot cost more than the $15 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:00.540
spent on each episode of the final season of&nbsp;
"Game of Thrones." Meanwhile, Deadline reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:04.080
that there were long delays and production&nbsp;
issues throughout the filming of the pilot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.720
including a complete re-edit when HBO executives&nbsp;
were unconvinced after seeing the first cut. HBO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:14.760
pulled the plug shortly after and locked the pilot&nbsp;
away so that no one would ever get to see it.

